From d074fecade9c15d1da7aabac51e0282364874b63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <neil.johnson71@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:44:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 23/26] asgb: ldh takes a full I/O address, in both directions

"ldh a,(nn)" tested the wrong variable:

    if ((t1 == S_R8) && (v1 == A) && (t2 == S_INDM)) {
        if (((v2 & 0xFF00) == 0x0000) ||
            ((v1 & 0xFF00) == 0xFF00)) {

v1 is the first operand, which in that branch is the A register, so
the second test could never be true: a full address - 0xFF42 rather
than the 0x42 offset - always fell through to "I/O Address Not In
Range 0x00-0xFF".

The store form immediately below has the same two tests against v1,
where v1 really is the address, and has always worked.  That
asymmetry is what makes this a typo rather than a rule.

The Game Boy's hardware registers are written as full addresses, so
SDCC and SDAS both emit them that way, and four cases of SDCC's sm83
suite fail to assemble without this.

astest/cases/gbldh pins each offset assembling the same from either
spelling, and that an address in neither page is still refused in
both directions - which is what the test was reaching for all along.
tgb.asm still assembles to the bytes its own comments give, all 1384.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 asgb/gbmch.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/asgb/gbmch.c b/asgb/gbmch.c
index 0d30c86..2af4e12 100644
--- a/asgb/gbmch.c
+++ b/asgb/gbmch.c
@@ -825,8 +825,19 @@ machine(struct mne *mp)
 
 		if ((t1 == S_R8) && (v1 == A) && (t2 == S_INDM)) {
 			if (is_abs(&e2)) {
+				/*
+				 * v2, not v1:  v1 is the A register
+				 * in this branch, so the test could
+				 * never be true and a full I/O
+				 * address - which is how the hardware
+				 * registers are written - was always
+				 * out of range.  "ldh (0xFF42),a"
+				 * below has the same two tests and
+				 * has always worked, which is what
+				 * makes it a typo rather than a rule.
+				 */
 				if (((v2 & 0xFF00) == 0x0000) ||
-			 	    ((v1 & 0xFF00) == 0xFF00)) {
+			 	    ((v2 & 0xFF00) == 0xFF00)) {
 					outab(0xF0);
 					outab(v2);
 				} else {
